---
import '../styles/case-studies.css';
---

<section id="case-studies" class="case-studies-layer shell" data-case-studies aria-labelledby="case-studies-title">
  <header class="case-studies-heading case-reveal">
    <div>
      <span class="case-index-label">02 / case studies</span>
      <h2 id="case-studies-title">The architecture<br />becomes concrete here.</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      The same operating system is easier to understand when each responsibility boundary is traced back to a real
      problem, a design decision, and something that has to keep working after deployment.
    </p>
  </header>

  <nav class="case-index case-reveal" aria-label="Featured case studies">
    <a href="#case-minecartainer" data-case-target="case-minecartainer">
      <span>01 / stateful runtime</span>
      <strong>Minecartainer</strong>
      <small>persistent-state safety</small>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
    </a>
    <a href="#case-mc-router" data-case-target="case-mc-router">
      <span>02 / network edge</span>
      <strong>mc-router</strong>
      <small>responsibility-boundary safety</small>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
    </a>
    <a href="#case-home-platform" data-case-target="case-home-platform">
      <span>03 / infrastructure</span>
      <strong>Home Kubernetes Platform</strong>
      <small>reproducible operations</small>
      <i aria-hidden="true">↓</i>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <article id="case-minecartainer" class="case-study case-reveal" data-case-study>
    <header class="case-study-header">
      <div>
        <span class="case-study-kind">01 / stateful workload lifecycle</span>
        <h3>Minecartainer</h3>
        <p class="case-study-summary">
          A Minecraft Java server container image designed around a simple asymmetry: containers are replaceable;
          persistent worlds are not.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study-question">
        <span>CORE QUESTION</span>
        <p>How do I automate a disposable runtime without letting that automation casually mutate long-lived state?</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-study-details">
      <section>
        <span>ORIGIN</span>
        <h4>Build independently instead of maintaining a fork.</h4>
        <p>
          The immediate trigger was practical: the official itzg image did not provide the dedicated OpenCL-capable
          runtime I needed for a C2ME OCL setup, while delivering mods into Kubernetes was becoming cumbersome. Rather
          than carry a long-lived fork, I started an independent image and let it evolve around my own operating model.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>PROBLEM</span>
        <h4>Startup convenience can become persistent-state risk.</h4>
        <p>
          Kubernetes replaces Pods routinely, but a Minecraft world may survive hundreds of those replacements.
          Implicit reinstall logic, guessed ownership, destructive mirroring, or a badly timed shutdown can therefore
          damage state that is far more valuable than the container running it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>DESIGN DECISIONS</span>
        <h4>Make dangerous transitions explicit and inspectable.</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Separate installation from runtime instead of treating every start as initialization.</li>
          <li>Record managed installation state and fail on incompatible configuration before replacement.</li>
          <li>Keep mods, plugins and configuration on an independent S3-compatible delivery lifecycle.</li>
          <li>Use Minecraft-aware RCON save/stop semantics inside Kubernetes termination budgets.</li>
          <li>Publish explicit Java runtime targets, including a separate GPU/OpenCL-capable target.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>OPERATION</span>
        <h4>Failure behavior is part of the runtime contract.</h4>
        <p>
          The maintained Kubernetes patterns assume persistent volumes, a single writer, conservative replacement,
          explicit readiness and bounded graceful shutdown. CI exercises unsafe and ambiguous states as well as the
          successful boot path, because rejecting an operation can be the correct result.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <footer class="case-study-footer">
      <div class="case-invariant">
        <span>OPERATIONAL INVARIANT</span>
        <strong>Treat the container as disposable. Treat the world as valuable.</strong>
      </div>
      <div class="case-proof">
        <span>DEMONSTRATES</span>
        <p>Stateful workload design · container lifecycle · Kubernetes semantics · failure-oriented testing</p>
      </div>
      <a class="case-source" href="https://github.com/AlexanderGG-0520/minecartainer">source ↗</a>
    </footer>
  </article>

  <article id="case-mc-router" class="case-study case-reveal" data-case-study>
    <header class="case-study-header">
      <div>
        <span class="case-study-kind">02 / protocol-aware network edge</span>
        <h3>mc-router</h3>
        <p class="case-study-summary">
          A lightweight Minecraft gateway that gives independent backends one public entry point without making the
          edge process own the entire gameplay session.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study-question">
        <span>CORE QUESTION</span>
        <p>What is the router actually allowed to own as routing, monitoring and lifecycle features keep growing?</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-study-details">
      <section>
        <span>ORIGIN</span>
        <h4>One public endpoint, several independent workloads.</h4>
        <p>
          I wanted multiple Minecraft servers on Kubernetes to remain separate workloads while sharing the standard
          public port. Exposing a new public port per server, forcing every server behind a larger gameplay proxy, or
          embedding routing into every server container all put the responsibility in the wrong place for that model.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>PROBLEM</span>
        <h4>Useful operational features can silently redefine routing.</h4>
        <p>
          The first hostname router was small. As Kubernetes discovery, STATUS behavior, monitoring, Transfer, Bedrock,
          voice routing and lifecycle hooks appeared, the harder problem became preserving which component was the
          source of truth for each decision.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>DESIGN DECISIONS</span>
        <h4>Inspect protocol only as deeply as the responsibility requires.</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Read the Java handshake to select a backend, then proxy the remaining TCP stream opaquely.</li>
          <li>Feed static YAML and Kubernetes discovery into the same route-snapshot model.</li>
          <li>Keep monitoring state from implicitly becoming routing authority.</li>
          <li>Keep workload lifecycle control external to route correctness.</li>
          <li>Add deeper protocol-aware components only where opaque forwarding is genuinely insufficient.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>OPERATION</span>
        <h4>Production regressions tightened the boundary.</h4>
        <p>
          STATUS and LOGIN behavior are now deliberately separated: explicit STATUS controls have a defined precedence,
          while LOGIN and Transfer keep using the normal backend. A production STATUS-routing regression reinforced the
          rule that observed availability must not silently become the source of truth for player traffic.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <footer class="case-study-footer">
      <div class="case-invariant">
        <span>DESIGN INVARIANT</span>
        <strong>Monitoring may describe routing state. It must not silently redefine routing state.</strong>
      </div>
      <div class="case-proof">
        <span>DEMONSTRATES</span>
        <p>Go networking · protocol design · concurrency · observability · explicit responsibility boundaries</p>
      </div>
      <a class="case-source" href="https://github.com/AlexanderGG-0520/mc-router">source ↗</a>
    </footer>
  </article>

  <article id="case-home-platform" class="case-study case-reveal" data-case-study>
    <header class="case-study-header">
      <div>
        <span class="case-study-kind">03 / infrastructure & operations</span>
        <h3>Home Kubernetes Platform</h3>
        <p class="case-study-summary">
          The operating platform underneath these projects: virtualization, cluster lifecycle, networking, storage and
          GitOps on hardware I actually administer.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study-question">
        <span>CORE QUESTION</span>
        <p>How do I turn one physical server into a platform whose infrastructure and workloads have separate lifecycles?</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-study-details">
      <section>
        <span>ORIGIN</span>
        <h4>A container experiment turned into a platform rebuild.</h4>
        <p>
          While running Minecraft on openSUSE, I discovered Docker and tried to adopt Compose V2. That path was awkward
          enough in the environment I had that I started looking at alternatives and found Kubernetes. If the platform
          was going to be rebuilt anyway, Proxmox + Kubernetes was more compelling than stacking a single-node control
          plane and all application workloads directly onto the host OS.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>PROBLEM</span>
        <h4>One physical machine should not mean one undifferentiated lifecycle.</h4>
        <p>
          Minecraft servers, web applications, storage, ingress and platform services all need different replacement
          and failure semantics. A bare-metal all-in-one node would work, but it would couple the Control Plane,
          application workloads and host maintenance into the same lifecycle boundary.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>DESIGN DECISIONS</span>
        <h4>Separate layers before adding physical scale.</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Use Proxmox as the virtualization boundary beneath Kubernetes.</li>
          <li>Run Control Plane and Worker roles in separate VMs even on the current single physical host.</li>
          <li>Use Kubernetes reconciliation as the common operating model for Minecraft and web workloads.</li>
          <li>Assign networking, storage, delivery and runtime responsibilities to Cilium, Longhorn, Argo CD and CRI-O.</li>
          <li>Keep the topology extensible so additional physical nodes do not require abandoning the current model.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <span>OPERATION</span>
        <h4>The learning loop ended in reproducibility, not a one-off install.</h4>
        <p>
          I first built the cluster with guidance, then kept operating and rebuilding it until I could reconstruct the
          platform myself and write deployment procedures for other people. Today it runs real Minecraft and web
          workloads. The current hardware is still one failure domain, so the design does not pretend that VM separation
          is high availability.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <footer class="case-study-footer">
      <div class="case-invariant">
        <span>PLATFORM PRINCIPLE</span>
        <strong>Separate lifecycle boundaries now; add physical failure domains when the hardware grows.</strong>
      </div>
      <div class="case-proof">
        <span>DEMONSTRATES</span>
        <p>Platform engineering · Kubernetes operations · virtualization · GitOps · storage & network design</p>
      </div>
      <span class="case-source case-source-muted">running system / no public infra repository</span>
    </footer>
  </article>

  <div class="case-studies-next case-reveal" aria-hidden="true">
    <span>03 / evidence & operation</span>
    <i></i>
    <span>deeper implementation follows</span>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import '../scripts/case-studies.ts';
</script>
